Revision history for Geo-Coder-DataScienceToolkit

0.04	Sat Oct 17 09:10:00 PM EDT 2026
	geocode(): the location rule is Geo::Location::Predicate's, which is a
		distribution of its own now, so this module declares it as a recommendation in
		META instead of requiring Geo::Coder::Free, and nothing here decides the rule
		again.  It is looked for when it is first needed rather than when this module
		loads, so importing this module reads no file it may not need.  Without it a
		reference is still refused and a string of digits is looked up as it was
		before the rule existed; with it the rule decides everything, and it is called
		by name rather than imported, which is what lets t/reference.t swap it for a
		spy and prove that it is the rule being consulted.
	[ CI ]
	.github/workflows/location-predicates.yml runs the location-predicate fuzz
		here, on a schedule and on a push, with --sibling
		Geo-Coder-DataScienceToolkit: this distribution is graded against
		Geo::Location::Predicate and the other five are not, so a drift here fails
		in the repository that would have to fix it rather than only in
		Geo-Coder-Free's daily run.
	new(ua => $ua) works, which is the calling form the POD documents.  It used
		to die with "Not a HASH reference": Object::Configure::configure() takes
		a hashref of the caller's values and was handed the argument list
		instead, so every call that passed a value failed.  A hashref of values
		is accepted as well, and an odd number of arguments is refused rather
		than silently dropped.
	t/10-new.t covers the constructor's calling conventions: no arguments, a
		caller's own agent, the same arguments in a hashref, that a key with no
		value is refused rather than read as an argument of its own, that the
		agent handed in is the agent the object then uses - the lookup is shown
		going through it, with a stub agent that answers instead of asking - and
		the ua() accessor's documented getter and setter.  The POD documents the
		hashref form and the refusal.

0.03	Sat Oct 17 07:58:42 PM EDT 2026
	geocode(): croak when input is just a set of numbers
	Use JSON::MaybeXS instead of JSON
	Use gtar to create a distribution on Macs
	Use Test::Needs and Object::Configure
	Use Test::DescribeMe to simplify tests
	Handle broken smokers
	geocode(): refuse a reference; it used to be accepted because the string it
		turns into contains a non-digit
	Check a reference against Geo::Location::Predicate's rule when that module
		is installed, so that the check cannot drift away from Geo::Coder::Free
	Added t/reference.t, the same test as the other Geo::Coder distributions

0.02	Sat Feb  2 08:02:49 EST 2019
	Documentation fixes

0.01	Fri Feb  1 14:59:14 EST 2019
        First version
